## Getting Started

### Prerequisites

- Node.js (v18+)
- MongoDB (local or Atlas)
- An SMTP email account (e.g., Gmail with an App Password) for sending OTPs

### 1. Clone the repo

```bash
git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/baseauth.git
cd baseauth
```

### 2. Install dependencies

```bash
# Backend
cd server
npm install

# Frontend
cd ../client
npm install
```

### 3. Environment Variables

Create a `.env` file in `server/`:

```env
MONGO_URI=
PORT=
NODE_ENV=

JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=
JWT_ACCESS_EXPIRES_IN=
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=
JWT_REFRESH_EXPIRES_IN=

GMAIL_USER=
GMAIL_APP_PASS=

FRONTEND_URL=
APP_NAME=
```

Create a `.env` file in `client/`:

```env
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000/api
```

### 4. Run the project

```bash
# Backend
cd server
npm run dev

# Frontend
cd client
npm run dev
```

## API Endpoints

### Auth

| Method | Endpoint | Description |
|---|---|---|
| POST | `/api/auth/register` | Register a new user |
| POST | `/api/auth/verify-account` | Verify email using OTP |
| POST | `/api/auth/login` | Log in |
| POST | `/api/auth/logout` | Log out & clear refresh token |
| POST | `/api/auth/rotate-token` | Rotate access token using refresh token |
| POST | `/api/auth/forgot-password` | Request OTP for password reset |
| POST | `/api/auth/reset-password` | Reset password using OTP |

### User (Protected — requires valid access token)

| Method | Endpoint | Description |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/api/user/get-me` | Get current user profile |
| PATCH | `/api/user/change-name` | Update name |
| PATCH | `/api/user/change-email` | Update email (triggers OTP re-verification) |
| PATCH | `/api/user/change-password` | Change password |
| PATCH | `/api/user/change-mobile` | Update phone number |
| DELETE | `/api/user/delete-account` | Delete account |

## Security Notes

- Access & refresh tokens are stored in **httpOnly, secure cookies** — never in localStorage.
- Refresh tokens use **rotation with reuse detection** to protect against token replay attacks.
- OTPs are time-bound and single-use.
- Passwords are hashed (e.g., bcrypt) before storage — never stored in plain text.

## Roadmap / Possible Extensions

- [ ] Rate limiting on OTP requests
- [ ] Social login (Google/GitHub OAuth)
- [ ] Role-based access control (RBAC)
- [ ] 2FA via authenticator app

